  "step_2": "The individual reports that the difference in amounts (2500 vs. 2400) is very small, so they prefer having a slightly higher chance of receiving any money at all. They explicitly compare the importance of receiving money (i.e., nonzero outcome) versus the amount received and state that they prioritized the chance of receiving something over a slightly higher amount. This indicates that the individual focused on the probability of a nonzero outcome (34% in B vs. 33% in A) and considered this small increase in probability more important than the 100 Euro difference in payoff. Their justification centers on maximizing the chance of a positive outcome, not on the shape of the distribution or skewness per se.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring more right-skewed lotteries, which in this case would mean choosing Lottery A (more probability on the unfavorable outcome). However, the individual chooses based on a higher chance of receiving money, which corresponds to Lottery B (34% vs. 33%). The information considered by the individual—probability of nonzero gain and comparison of monetary amounts—does not align with the concept of skewness as defined in the reason. Moreover, the individual’s justification is about prioritizing the likelihood of a positive outcome, which is the opposite of favoring right-skewness (where unfavorable outcomes are more likely). Therefore, the individual is not using the skewness-based reason; in fact, they are acting contrary to it. The comparison of relevant information and decision rules shows no alignment with the stated reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
